{"name":"Fozzie Bear","hex":"#70625c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bear","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large, generally omnivorous mammal (a few species are purely carnivorous or herbivorous) of the family Ursidae; having shaggy fur, a very small tail, and flat feet.\n The meat of this animal.","The meat of this animal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To endeavour to depress the price of, or prices in."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bear"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fozzie Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Fozzie Bear is a Muppet character from the sketch comedy television series The Muppet Show, best known as the insecure and comedically fruitless stand-up comic. Fozzie is an anthropomorphic orange-brown bear who often wears a brown pork pie hat and a pink and white polka dot necktie. The character debuted on The Muppet Show, as the series' resident comedian, a role where he uses the catchphrase \"Wocka wocka!\" to indicate that he had completed a joke. He was often the target of ridicule, particularly from balcony hecklers Statler and Waldorf. Fozzie was performed by Frank Oz until 2000, after which Eric Jacobson became the character's principal performer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fozzie_Bear_puppet_in_1979.jpg/330px-Fozzie_Bear_puppet_in_1979.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fozzie_Bear"},{"title":"Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae. They are classified as caniforms within the suborder Caniformia. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg/330px-Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/500px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/1280px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/500px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUrsidae_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG/500px-Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG/500px-Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plithocyon armagnacensis skull, a member of the extinct subfamily Hemicyoninae from the Miocene","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/500px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/1280px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wik